{"author":{"id":"b409816c5cd5ab140d1d52eb33aace35de44952bb794b19806fd57f6ecb1ace9"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1790363200,"nonce":"mFsDf1G9WPOpujk/BQT1Ex86fh4=","title":"zylua aborts in cranelift-jit's compiled_blob.rs:142 (TryFromIntError PosOverflow) on official constructs.lua, x86_64 Linux release","message":"A release zylua on the NUC (x86_64 Linux) running the Lua 5.4.9 suite's constructs.lua in the per-file official configuration (zylua run -e \"_U=true _soft=true _port=true _nomsg=true\" constructs.lua, warm-up on, Cranelift tiers) panics after printing its first sections:\n\n    thread '\u003cunnamed\u003e' panicked at .../cranelift/jit/src/compiled_blob.rs:142:80:\n    called `Result::unwrap()` on an `Err` value: TryFromIntError(PosOverflow)\n    fatal runtime error: failed to initiate panic, error 5, aborting\n\nand exits 134. That line narrows a relocation's PC-relative distance to 32 bits: two pieces of JIT code, or code and a symbol it calls, ended up more than 2 GB apart. constructs.lua loads and compiles tens of thousands of small chunks, and each compiled function takes a fresh code region (03a8575), so the regions spread over the address space.\n\nRates, 2 runs each: main 588a2748 1 of 2, a6eae1e7 1 of 2, 35ddc143 2 of 2 (release, llvm-backend feature, LLVM tier off). A debug build with warm-up off passed its one run (120 s). Not seen on the Mac so far.\n\nFix direction: allocate JIT code from one reserved range (a memory provider that carves regions from a single reservation), or make the relocations the backend emits absolute where the distance cannot be bounded.","files":null}]}